0 @N1822@ NOTE We undertook the arduous task of deciphering the handwritten page
1 CONC s of the 1940 Census to create a searchable index for the census. This w
1 CONC as accomplished gradually, state by state, as we covered more and more o
1 CONC f the census.As required by the US Constitution, the census is a federa
1 CONC l mandate to count every resident of the United States of America ever
1 CONC y 10 years. Census data is released to the public 72 years after it wa
1 CONC s taken.Federal census takers were asked to record information about eve
1 CONC ry person who was in each household on the census day. A census taker mi
1 CONC ght have visited a house on a later date, but the information he collect
1 CONC ed was supposed to be about the people who were in the house on the cens
1 CONC us day. The basic census enumeration unit was the county. Each county wa
1 CONC s divided into enumeration districts, one for each enumerator. The compl
1 CONC eted forms were sent to the Commerce Department's Census Office in Washi
1 CONC ngton, D.C.Federal censuses are usually reliable, depending on the knowl
1 CONC edge of the informant and the care of the census enumerator. Informatio
1 CONC n may have been given to a census taker by any member of the family or b
1 CONC y a neighbor. Some information may have been incorrect or deliberately f
1 CONC alsified.
0 @N1823@ NOTE What can you find in the  census?Census returns can help you determ
1 CONC ine who your ancestors were, and can also tell you:- Where your ancestor
1 CONC s were living- Who they were living with- What their occupations were- I
1 CONC f they had any servants- Who their neighbours were- If they had any brot
1 CONC hers and sisters- What their ages were at the time of the census- If the
1 CONC y had any disabilities.As well as giving you the above information, th
1 CONC e fact that census returns are taken every ten years also allows you t
1 CONC o track the movements of your ancestors through time as they perhaps mov
1 CONC e house, get married, have children or even change occupations.The field
1 CONC s which have been transcribed for the  census are:- First name- Middle n
1 CONC ame- Last name- Sex- Birth place- Age- Place of residence- County- Relat
1 CONC ionship to head of householdWhy this collection is so valuableCensus rec
1 CONC ords are valuable since they can tell you where a person lived at a cert
1 CONC ain place and time. Censuses were conducted by the federal government an
1 CONC d will offer a variety of information, depending on year. Census record
1 CONC s can answer questions like where your ancestors were living at the tim
1 CONC e the census was taken, who they were living with, what their occupation
1 CONC s were, who their neighbors were, if they had any brothers and sisters
1 CONC , what their ages were at the time of the census and if they had any dis
1 CONC abilities.Searching the censusThe golden rule of family history is to ch
1 CONC eck the original historical record, or 'primary source', wherever possib
1 CONC le. We have provided clear images of the original census enumeration boo
1 CONC ks for you to view once you've found the right family in the indexes. Wh
1 CONC en using census returns you should first search the transcriptions to he
1 CONC lp locate your ancestor in the census, and then view the original image
1 CONC s to validate your findings. It will also help you see the household i
1 CONC n the context of surrounding households. This is particularly importan
1 CONC t as transcribing an entire census is a huge and difficult task, and whi
1 CONC lst we have used the expertise of our transcribers and the experience o
1 CONC f key representatives from the genealogy community to help us translat
1 CONC e the records, it is inevitable that there will be some errors.Next step
1 CONC sWith the information you gain from these census records, you will hav
1 CONC e the information you need to search for vital records in the locality w
1 CONC here you found your ancestor. Also, the fact that census returns are tak
1 CONC en every ten years also allows you to track the movements of our ancesto
1 CONC rs through time as they perhaps move house, get married, have children o
1 CONC r even change occupations.
0 @N1824@ NOTE The consolidated records in this collection often tell a wider stor
1 CONC y about a person: They allow a researcher to learn when a person marrie
1 CONC d or divorced, and in some cases deduce when the head of the household o
1 CONC r their spouse died. They also help track changes in workplace or occupa
1 CONC tion and reveal when a person moved from one location to another. City d
1 CONC irectories, like census records, contain information that helps genealog
1 CONC ists establish residences, occupations, and relationships between indivi
1 CONC duals. The added benefit of city directories is that they were publishe
1 CONC d annually in many cities and towns throughout the United States. MyHeri
1 CONC tage corrected errors in the original Optical Character Recognition (OCR
1 CONC ) scans of the directory pages, and then employed several advanced techn
1 CONC ologies, including Record Extraction, Name Entity Recognition, and Condi
1 CONC tional Random Fields to parse the data, and correct errors in the origin
1 CONC al OCR output of the directory pages. Training a machine learning mode
1 CONC l how to parse raw free-text records into names, occupations, and addres
1 CONC ses enabled the production of a structured, searchable index of valuabl
1 CONC e historical information. Optical Character Recognition may introduce mi
1 CONC stakes in some records, and the use of machine learning to parse these r
1 CONC ecords may create mistakes as well. Therefore, as with any genealogica
1 CONC l record, users are encouraged to consult the original images and fix an
1 CONC y mistakes they may find when extracting information into their family t
1 CONC rees. The records in this collection date back to the 1800s and are an e
1 CONC xcellent resource for creating a more informed picture of family life du
1 CONC ring the intervening years between censuses. Of particular note is the s
1 CONC trength of city directories in filling in the genealogical gap caused b
1 CONC y the destruction of almost all of the 1890 U.S. Federal Census schedule
1 CONC s. The 20-year period between the 1880 Census and the 1900 Census has lo
1 CONC ng been a challenge in family history research, and city directories o
1 CONC n MyHeritage from this period serve as an important census substitute. C
1 CONC ity directories were first published in the U.S. in 1785, with directori
1 CONC es from Philadelphia, New York City, Boston, and Baltimore being publish
1 CONC ed before the end of the 18th century. The popularity of these directori
1 CONC es increased tremendously in the 19th century and reached their zenith d
1 CONC uring the latter part of the 20th century. Publishers of these directori
1 CONC es employed local residents to canvas these cities and towns regularly t
1 CONC o collect and update the data they included in their publications. The i
1 CONC nformation collected varies somewhat by year and publisher as practice
1 CONC s evolved. For example, by the start of the 20th century, it was commo
1 CONC n to find the names of deceased spouses listed. Some publishers even col
1 CONC lected and published the names of recently deceased residents with thei
1 CONC r age at death and full death date. City directories were most commonl
1 CONC y published under the name of a primary city but often contain the sam
1 CONC e information for nearby smaller cities and towns. This collection wil
1 CONC l be updated soon to include pre-1860 directories as well as a large an
1 CONC d unique set of directories published after 1960.
0 @N1825@ NOTE Birth information may be included for those residents born primaril
1 CONC y between 1900 and 1990. The original sources are not available.
0 @N1826@ NOTE Note that some records are pre-1900. Cemetery records are especiall
1 CONC y helpful for identifying ancestors who were not recorded in other recor
1 CONC ds, such as children who died young or women.
