0 @N2045@ NOTE During the majority of the 19th century, numerous obituaries foun
1 CONC d in American newspapers were essentially written replicas of tombston
1 CONC e inscriptions, primarily consisting of names, birthdates, and death dat
1 CONC es. During the American Civil War, obituaries expanded to provide additi
1 CONC onal biographical information about fallen soldiers in order to reach a
1 CONC s many relatives as possible. In the late 1900s, amid the Industrial Rev
1 CONC olution, obituaries began to include details such as the deceased's fina
1 CONC ncial standing, occupation, and years of employment. Local newspaper obi
1 CONC tuaries frequently provide information regarding funeral and burial arra
1 CONC ngements for the deceased.A standardized obituary template was establish
1 CONC ed by funeral homes and newspapers in the 1930s. This template typicall
1 CONC y consisted of four parts: a death announcement, a brief biography wit
1 CONC h a list of survivors, and funeral details.The records in this collectio
1 CONC n were extracted using machine learning technology and may contain mista
1 CONC kes due to Optical Character Recognition (OCR) technology and the automa
1 CONC tic extraction. Users are encouraged to fix such mistakes using the “Sug
1 CONC gest an alternative” feature that is found on every record page. Image
1 CONC s for these newspapers can be viewed on OldNews.com.
0 @N2046@ NOTE The images are split by borough, bride and groom, and then sorted a
1 CONC lphabetically. We have linked the bride and groom together, when possibl
1 CONC e, using the license number.Marriage records are an important genealogic
1 CONC al record as they not only record information about the bride, the groom
1 CONC , and their residence when the marriage occurred but often contain addit
1 CONC ional information such as birth dates, birth places, occupations, and wh
1 CONC ether single, widowed, or divorced at the time of the marriage. Ofte
1 CONC n a marriage license will also contain information about the parents o
1 CONC f the bride and groom including the parents’ names and birthplaces. Copi
1 CONC es of the original marriage records in New York City are available for o
1 CONC rder for a fee from the Office of the City Clerk. Marriages 50 or more y
1 CONC ears old are classified as public documents and are available to all res
1 CONC earchers. Marriages less than 50 years old are restricted and only avail
1 CONC able under certain circumstances that are outlined at the webpage linke
1 CONC d to above.
